/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '650,705p' docs/decisions.md; rg -n 'OD99 \\||OD106 \\||OD114 \\||OD126 \\||OD147 \\|' docs/decisions.md; rg -n 'shift|remainder|MIN|OutOfRange|STMT-4|assignment|operand|byte column|column' legacy/docs/spec/{arithmetic,statements,types}.md ConcreteSpec/Spec/*.lean" in /tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/found5/codex
 succeeded in 0ms:
    accepted today with an input-dependent trap, becomes a refused example with accepted guarded
    variants.
  - *Rows revised.* [OD10](#od10) and ARITH-3 (checked by default with an abort becomes: evidence
    required, named forms for the rest), MEM-6 (abort out of range becomes: evidence required), MEM-9,
    ARITH-13 and [OD118](#od118) (refusal of proven violations is subsumed by refusal of unproven
    safety), Q4.6 and Q4.7 (`byte_at` and the range form without `else` need evidence; the open
    question on refusing a provably out-of-range view index disappears), Q6.1 and Q6.2 (static refusal
    widens from proven violations to unproven safety; known values remain facts), [OD97](#od97) (the
    profile option is subsumed), and ARITH-9: `#[overflow_checked]` is retired (owner, 2026-11-06, relayed by the orchestrator from
    the owner session: "ok do everything", approving the unanimous recommendation of Claude, Codex,
    agy and Kimi to retire it as obsolete under no hidden traps). Qualified: CON-2, Q9.11 and [OD125](#od125) (a claim used for safety
    admission needs closed evidence), CON-6 and [OD80](#od80) (`assume` cannot admit an operation in
    safe code), [OD120](#od120) (a vacuous precondition used for safety is refused), [OD82](#od82)
    (extended to functions), Q7.10 and Q8.5 (extended by the stack target-contract obligations of Q14, which are future target work, not current guarantees). Unchanged: STMT-1, STMT-2, OD101 (general trap semantics), Q6.3–Q6.6
    (with the interactions stated in the proposal), CON-1 and P18.
  - *Spellings* (owner, 2026-11-06: "ok agreed with the 3 decisions"; checked by Codex, agy and Kimi,
    whose shared refinements are recorded here under the owner's rule that what Claude and Codex agree
    on is recorded).
    1. *Checked operations*, in [OD99](#od99)'s family: `checked_add`, `checked_sub`, `checked_mul`,
       `checked_div`, `checked_rem`, `checked_shl`, `checked_shr` and `checked_neg`, each returning
       `Result<T, ArithmeticError>` with `ArithmeticError { Overflow, DivisionByZero, InvalidShift }`.
       Signed `MIN / -1` and `MIN % -1` give `Overflow`. `checked_shl` and `checked_shr` fail only on an
       invalid shift count (`InvalidShift`); a valid shift keeps the decided bit-discarding semantics.
       `ArithmeticError` is separate from `try_convert`'s `OutOfRange` ([OD106](#od106)).
    2. *Naming a Lean theorem*: `#[proof(path::to::theorem)]`, repeatable, on the function
       declaration. The path resolves to a typed theorem identity, never a string (rule 13), and the
       axiom and dependency checks stay mandatory. The theorem's role follows from its statement:
       (a) a statement of obligation form for this function that exactly equals a current generated
       obligation discharges it, and every discharge edge is recorded, each obligation identity with
       its own edge even when several obligations are identical, never by first match; (b) a statement
       of obligation form that matches no current obligation (stale or mistyped) is refused with a
       diagnostic, never reclassified; (c) any other statement must refer to the annotated function's
       current semantic identity or its generated semantic contract, and is recorded in the facts as a
       proven property; (d) a theorem unrelated to the function (for example one proving `True`) is
       refused.
    3. *The termination permission*: `#[may_not_terminate("reason")]`, with a mandatory, non-empty
       reason. It attaches before a `while` loop; on one designated function per recursive component,
       waiving only that component's cycle obligation (a second marker for the same component is
       refused); on an extern or blocking declaration; or as a prefix to an expression, where the
       checker requires the target to be an indirect call. It never relaxes [OD115](#od115):
       specification functions stay total. Grammar, which must stay LL(1) and is checked mechanically:
       a statement factors one leading attribute-prefix production before branching on loop or
       expression statement; an expression attribute attaches to the immediately following postfix
       expression (so `#[may_not_terminate("r")] fp(x) + g()` attaches to `fp(x)`); the checker
       classifies the targets. This extends [OD82](#od82). The grammar data in ConcreteSpec is updated
       in the spec work (A2i).
- <a id="decided-project-core-statement"></a>**The core statement** (owner, 2026-11-06: "concrete core
  idea is to be fully proven system programming language with no gc where i can know everything").
  README.md and PHILOSOPHY.md open with it: Concrete is a fully proven systems programming language
  with no garbage collector, where you can know everything a program does: what it does, what it may
  touch, whether it can fail or stop, and what is proved about it. "Fully proven" means the decided
  proof model ([proof infrastructure](#decided-project-proof-infrastructure)): compiler correctness
  is proved end to end, with every unproven step named as an explicit assumption and never silently
  trusted, and a program's own correctness is proved by Lean theorems about it. The statement says
  what the language is for, not what is proved today: each guarantee holds only as its evidence
  lands, compiler correctness and program correctness need separate proofs, and native claims remain
1214:| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
1221:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
1229:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
1241:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles |
1262:| OD147 | <a id="od147"></a>`repr(C, packed)` together (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `#[repr(C, packed)]` is accepted, and what layout it gives. Bears on FFI-4 and FFI-8. The current compiler: accepted: `#[repr(C, packed)] struct Copy P { a: u8, b: u32 }` has size 5, so packing overrides C alignment. FFI-4 refuses only `packed` with `align(N)`, and FFI-8 states C layout and packed layout separately. Options: (a) refuse the combination; (b) accept it as C field order with no padding and alignment 1, as the current compiler does, and FFI-8 says so. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-147 | see OPEN-147 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept: C order, no padding, align 1, stated in FFI-8 |
