<a id="TYPE-3"></a>
### TYPE-3. `Text` is owned

**TYPE-3@2.** A `Text` owns an immutable copy of its bytes, never aliases another value's buffer, and is linear.

- Kind: guarantee
- Uses: none
- Codes: none
- Status: pending R-0555
- Enforced by: std/src/text.con#Text
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: none
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** Ownership of immutable text remains required, but the literal/static-text type and
its conversion to allocating strings are [undecided: see the target Core proposal](decisions.md#core-static-text).
The old allocating library example is retired until it can be stated without choosing that API.

<a id="TYPE-4"></a>
### TYPE-4. One type per expression, no implicit conversion

**TYPE-4@2.** Every expression has exactly one type, and every position a value flows into (a binding, an argument, a return, a field, a branch or arm of a value-producing `if` or `match`) expects that type exactly, with no implicit conversion, except that an expression that never completes (a diverging `return`, `break`, `continue` or call) fits any position other than a condition (TYPE-5).

- Kind: refusal
- Uses: none
- Codes: E0220 (check), E0222 (check), E0224 (check), E0225 (check), E0226 (check), E0500 (core-check), E0560 (core-check)
- Status: enforced
# Reading the Concrete specification

**Status: PROPOSED.** This is the specification of the new compiler, written in Lean (decision
rows Q9.15, P3: Lean is the specification). It is not normative until the owner freezes Core at
stage A2c (row Q9.19). Before that, the owner approved the Core design provisionally (A2a,
2026-11-05), and A2b formalized it here and checked the four design theorems (row Q9.14).

## How to read it

[`Spec.lean`](Spec.lean) is the specification library (row Q9.15; split into modules by owner
decision 2026-11-06). It imports the modules under [`Spec/`](Spec/) in reading order, and each
module's header says what it covers. Every rule is a Lean definition with a plain-English doc
comment, followed by example programs: the surface program in a doc comment, its Core form, and
what the rules say about it: accepted (`check p = true`) with its outcome (`run p 10 = .exit 42`),
or refused (`check p = false`). You can read it without reading any proof.

[`Proofs/`](Proofs/) says why the rules hang together. Read it only to check a claim.

**The owner view.** `scripts/spec/lean_site.sh [OUT]` (after `lake build ConcreteSpec`) generates
a static HTML view into `_build/spec-site/`: every module rendered from its source (doc comments as
prose, rules and examples as code with linked names), each declaration with what it uses and which
proofs are about it, and a page of all theorems with their statements. It is generated from the
sources and the elaborated environment only, and fails on a broken link.

The decisions are cited by row id (Q1.1 … Q9.19, P1 … P23, in
[docs/decisions.md](../docs/decisions.md)) and by rule id (ODnn, TYPE-n, OWN-n, …, in the
archived prose specification [legacy/docs/spec](../legacy/docs/spec)).

## Rules of the file

- Only Lean core is imported, no Mathlib (Q9.16). No `sorry`, no `native_decide` (Q9.17), and
  `autoImplicit` is off, so a misspelled name is an error, never a silently bound variable.
- Axioms: only Lean's standard `propext`, `Quot.sound` and `Classical.choice`. The evaluator
  `run`, the checker `check` and the other executable definitions use `propext` alone, so they
  stay executable and the examples are decided by running them; `Classical.choice` appears only
  in proofs (`rep_roundtrip` through the core lemma `Nat.mod_mul`, and the range proofs).
- These rules are checked in CI by [scripts/proofs](../scripts/proofs): the per-theorem axiom
  inventory against its golden list, the transitive ban on `sorry` and `native_decide`, a fresh
  kernel replay with `leanchecker`, and an independent re-check of the exported theorems by
  con-leche and nanoda.
- The judgments (inductive relations) are the normative form; the checker is proved to agree with
  them (`check_iff`).
- Build: `lake build ConcreteSpec`. The external checker re-checks every theorem.

## What remains open

The parts are separate Core fragments, each defined only as far as its theorems need (R4 F8);
unifying them into one Core is A2i work (R-0571).

Open obligations, by name:

- **O1 Elaboration** from surface syntax to Core: how literals get a type, how names become
  identities, how nested expressions and grouping become A-normal form, source positions, and that
  acceptance of a source program does not depend on how the elaborator names temporaries.
- **O2 The lexer, model-translation and native-image obligations.**
- **O3 Instrumentation erasure** (Q9.10): the ownership instrumentation (`Slot.moved`, the
  `used`, `live`, `known` and `kfields` fields of `Entry`) is stated as intent, with no erasure
  definition or theorem yet. (Brand erasure, part 5, is done.)
- **O4 Trap diagnostics** (Q7.10, OD101): the bytes of the trap line, including the trap's source
  location (needs O1's positions), and of the startup refusal.
- **O5 Target observations** (the launcher contract): every result here is about the model's
  outcomes; a claim about an executable needs a transport to the approved launcher contract.
- **O6 Launch validation** (part 6): `ProgOk` assumes a valid launch: instance limits, the target
  offering each parameter type, and the launcher's grant minting are not modelled; nor are
  authority rule 11, foreign calls, trait dispatch, generic methods and generated cleanup.
- **O7 Capabilities in the other parts**: the cleanup calls of part 3 and the allocator
  operations of part 5 need capabilities; part 6 checks capabilities in its own fragment only
  (unification, A2i).
- **O8 Part 3** abstracts each deferred call to its identity, dependencies, loans and whether it
  traps; it does not model the statements between registrations or evaluating a `return` value
  before cleanup.
- **O9 Part 4**: the run-time fragment (`vrun`) and the access rules (`Ok`) are separate
  fragments, not yet joined in one soundness theorem (A2i); the run covers array sources (text
  views share the index arithmetic, and their validity is `text_view_utf8`); views of views,
  writes through text views, and text whose length is known while its bytes are not (treated as
  unknown) are not modelled; the frame theorem covers bodies that access only their view. That
  compiled code performs the same range check is a compiler-slice obligation (O5).
- **O10 Part 5** abstracts the condition of `while`: loop test number `k` takes the outcome
  `σ k`, and the results hold for every `σ`.
it cannot turn a pointer into an integer (TRUST-1) or read through a raw pointer without `Unsafe`
(TRUST-4's `raw-deref-needs-unsafe`). Trusted
code that walks a buffer may also rely on the stride of an array of `T` being `sizeof::<T>()`
(FFI-12). An enum takes no `repr`; OD110 refuses enums at the foreign boundary (E0574).

```con accept name=observed-through-sizeof exit=42



struct Copy S { a: u8, b: u32 }

pub fn main() -> u8 {
    if sizeof::<S>() >= 5 && alignof::<S>() >= 1 {
        return 42;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

<a id="FFI-8"></a>
### FFI-8. What `repr(C)` and `repr(packed)` promise

**FFI-8@2.** `#[repr(C)]` lays a struct out as C does, each field in declaration order at the next offset aligned for its type and the size rounded up to the struct's alignment, and `#[repr(packed)]` places the fields in declaration order with no padding and gives the struct alignment 1, accepts misaligned fields but refuses references to them (also when combined with `repr(C)`).

- Kind: guarantee
- Uses: none
- Codes: none
- Status: pending R-0555
- Enforced by: Concrete.Layout.tySize, Concrete.Layout.tyAlign, Concrete.Layout.fieldOffset
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: layout comes from the one `Layout` authority (FFI-2, R-0502)
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** `repr(C)` is what a struct needs to cross `extern fn` (FFI-3), and `repr(packed)`
describes wire formats. `#[repr(align(N))]` promises only a minimum alignment, and today only where
it changes nothing (FFI-4, E0585). Which combinations are well formed is FFI-4. OD111 accepts packed fields at misaligned offsets and refuses references to them; the current compiler still accepts such references.

```con accept name=c-and-packed exit=42



#[repr(C)]
struct Copy R { v: u8, l: u32 }
#[repr(C, packed)]
struct Copy H { l: u32, f: u16, v: u8 }
#[repr(packed)]
struct Copy P { l: u32, v: u8 }

pub fn main() -> u8 {
    if sizeof::<R>() == 8 && alignof::<R>() == 4 && sizeof::<H>() == 7 && alignof::<H>() == 1
        && sizeof::<P>() == 5 && alignof::<P>() == 1 {
        return 42;
    }
    return 0;
}
```



```con accept name=misaligned-packed-field-value exit=42
#[repr(packed)]
struct Copy P { tag: u8, value: u32 }
fn main() -> u8 {
    let p: P = P { tag: 0, value: 42 };
    if p.value == 42 { return 42; }
    return 0;
}
```

<a id="FFI-9"></a>
### FFI-9. What `trusted extern fn` asserts

codex
S1 — **DISAGREE.** Cite a dated owner answer for “option b,” or mark it proposed. The authoritative [A2i decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06) says to freeze integrated scopes, “not an untested whole-language architecture.” It does not establish the claimed approval of this complete shared foundation.

C1 — **AGREE.**

C2 — **DISAGREE.** Label the exact `TrapKind` inventory as a new proposal requiring explicit approval. Q6.7 decides separation of traps, stuckness, OOM and fuel exhaustion; it does not decide these constructors. The arithmetic decision fixes `ArithmeticError`, which does not by itself fix the surrounding trap taxonomy.

C3 — **DISAGREE.** Separate owned `Text` from `StaticStr` and borrowed views. [TYPE-3](legacy/docs/spec/types.md#TYPE-3) makes `Text` an owning linear value; Q4.5 specifies an explicit FFI adapter, not every text type’s internal representation. A universal pointer-plus-length representation must account for C5’s release context or become an explicit owner question.

C4 — **DISAGREE.** Restrict padding independence to typed observations, and specify raw observation separately. The contract permits observing initialized raw bytes but also says padding cannot affect observations. Initialized padding copied to output contradicts that blanket law. State which padding choices representation simulations preserve or permit as nondeterminism.

C5 — **AGREE.**

C6 — **DISAGREE.** Mark byte columns and tab counting as proposed choices. The [runtime decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) specifies only 1-based columns. Also define whether the concurrency projection compares complete single-thread executions or projected segments; deleting synchronization events alone cannot remove interference from another thread.

C7 — **DISAGREE.** Specify the release-policy interface and its noninterference obligation before freezing. “Equal leakage under the declared release policy” leaves the central exception undefined. Include allocation sizes and loop counts explicitly, as [R-0592](ROADMAP.md) requires. Otherwise later secret and allocation slices must supply missing premises to foundational security theorems.

C8 — **AGREE.**

C9 — **AGREE.**

C10 — **AGREE.**

S3 — **DISAGREE.** Explicitly retain kernel-checked per-compilation certificates connecting exact source bytes to emitted BIR and binding backend input. Universal pass proofs and an image validator do not establish that the compiler executable performed those passes. Also require maximal-behaviour refinement across every lowering, including progress against infinite silent target stuttering; C6’s source-level observation law alone is insufficient.

REG — **DISAGREE with OD126 and early-evidence backend obligations; AGREE with all other rows.**

- **OD126:** Distinguish unchanged existing profiles from revised ones. The proposed profile interface does not inherently require changing existing little-endian/64-bit profiles; compact enum tags do. Specify which profile guarantees change so approval does not accidentally authorize unspecified replacements.
- **Early-evidence backend obligations:** Include the corresponding [Q8.1](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core) milestone revision. It explicitly chooses “proved source→BIR; qualified native; conditional image claim.” Advancing native closure to 0.1 should say exactly what stronger endpoint becomes mandatory and which conditional premises remain.

DEC1 — **DISAGREE.** Approve a corrected contract, rather than declaring this version complete. Resolve padding observations, owning-text representation, release policies, certificate transport and maximal-behaviour obligations first. Those affect theorem statements and cannot safely be deferred as instance details.

DEC2 — **DISAGREE.** Split independent changes into separately answerable approvals: target abstraction, `usize`, enum layout, release-context erasure, and registry/schema extensibility. They have different compatibility consequences. The owner should be able to accept the target model without also accepting every representation and source-type change.

DEC3 — **DISAGREE.** Offer “tagged layout now; future niche changes require explicit approval” instead of only permanent prohibition or permanent restriction to one niche. Freeze the representation interface and validity laws now. A forever-exhaustive optimization policy is unnecessary to those proofs and conflicts with the proposal’s extensible-interface principle.

DEC4 — **DISAGREE.** Separate final-image validation’s milestone from differential qualification. Qualification is independently useful and largely already required; moving machine-code closure to 0.1 is a substantial scheduling decision. Approve it against a scoped validator design and workload justification, not as a condition bundled with qualification.

ANSWER: S1=DISAGREE; C=2,3,4,6,7; S3=DISAGREE; REG=OD126,early-evidence backend obligations; DEC=DISAGREE,DISAGREE,DISAGREE,DISAGREE; UNFLAGGED=claimed option-b approval,byte-column/tab convention,exact TrapKind inventory,owned Text representation
