legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:35:### CON-2. What `requires`, `ensures`, `invariant` and `variant` claim
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:37:**CON-2@1.** `#[requires(p)]` claims that `p` holds at every call (an obligation of each caller, assumed on entry), `#[ensures(q)]` claims that `q` holds at every return with `result` naming the returned value, and a loop's `#[invariant(i)]` and `#[variant(v)]` claim that `i` holds before the loop and after every iteration and that `v` is non-negative and decreases on every iteration.
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:49:initialization, preservation, exit-to-postcondition, variant non-negativity and decrease), each
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:59:    #[invariant(0 <= i && i <= n)]
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:87:**CON-3@2.** Contract expressions are pure non-consuming specifications with mathematical integer arithmetic unless a machine operation is named; function clauses see parameters, permitted declarations and `result` only in `ensures`, postconditions read surviving parameter state after deferred cleanup without an implicit entry snapshot, and loop clauses and assertions use their own lexical scope including permitted ghost locals.
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:229:    #[invariant(0 <= i && i <= bound)]
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:261:    #[invariant(0 <= i && i <= limit)]
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:379:### CON-7. `result` is bound only in a postcondition
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:381:**CON-7@1.** `result` names the returned value in an `#[ensures]` clause and is bound in no other contract clause, so a `requires`, `invariant` or `variant` that names it names an unknown variable.
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:392:**Rationale.** Only a postcondition is evaluated after a return. A precondition that names `result`
legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md:424:**CON-8@1.** Several `#[requires]` clauses on one function, or several `#[invariant]` clauses on one loop, claim their conjunction, each clause becoming an obligation of its own.
