/bin/bash -lc "cat > /tmp/revise_foundation.py <<'PY'
from pathlib import Path
p=Path('_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL_v1.md').read_text()
p=p.replace('# Concrete Lean foundation proposal','# Concrete Lean foundation proposal — draft 2',1)
a=p.index('Status:'); b=p.index('\\nReferences below')
p=p[:a]+'''Status: proposal, not a completed freeze or approval of new rules. On 2026-11-07 the owner said, verbatim, **\"ok to option b then for freeze the foundation>?\"** (owner answer supplied with this revision). This approves writing the shared foundation before freezing it; **pending recording in docs/decisions.md**. It does not approve the new questions in section 5.

The [A2i decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06) freezes integrated scopes, \"not an untested whole-language architecture\". Here the frozen foundation means interfaces and laws implemented, proved and tested together, with representative interactions. It does not mean designing every future slice before slice 1. Later slices supply lawful instances; changes to decided rules return to the owner.
'''+p[b:]
p=p.replace('Each contract states the frozen interface and laws, its recommended rule, its authority, and the later work it permits.','These are contracts to complete, not declarations that the proofs exist. Decided rules remain binding. New semantic completions and revisions are separately answerable in section 5; none is approved by this draft.')
p=p.replace('Arrays destroy elements in increasing index order then backing; OrderedMap uses ascending keys, key before value, then nodes.','Collection destruction order is deferred under question 13.')
p=p.replace('Character validity excludes non-Unicode-scalars.','TYPE-20 makes char a one-byte code unit: every u8 round-trips through char. OD137 restricts character literals to ASCII; it does not restrict stored char values to ASCII.')
p=p.replace('with checked conversion returning "'`OutOfRange`.'"','with the checked-conversion error type left to question 12.')
a=p.index('Use exactly **"'`TrapKind'"'); b=p.index('\\n\\n**Authority:**',a)
p=p[:a]+'''Keep Q6.7's separation of traps, unsafe stuckness, allocation failure and evaluator fuel exhaustion. Handled checked errors are values, not traps; explicit abort is terminal. Safe admission requires evidence excluding implicit traps. The exact trap taxonomy and UTF-8 failure mapping are questions 9 and 11, not consequences of ArithmeticError.
'''+p[b:]
p=p.replace('Above Memory CFG, semantics uses structured values and never depends on layout.','Core uses the hybrid state in C4, with target/layout as a read-only parameter for sizeof/alignof and raw access. Safe typed operations are proved representation-independent where their premises permit it.')
p=p.replace('Text/StaticStr and views are pointer plus length; text length counts UTF-8 bytes.','StaticStr and views use their decided pointer/length adapters; text length counts UTF-8 bytes. TYPE-3 Text owns immutable bytes, never aliases another value’s buffer, and is linear. Its ownership, allocator dependency and sufficient release context must survive representation; Q4.5’s FFI adapter does not fix Text’s internal size. No universal two-word Text layout is frozen.')
a=p.index('Native enums use'); b=p.index('\\nInclude native, C and packed',a)
p=p[:a]+'''Native enum layout and niche policy are separate questions 4 and 5. Until answered, retain the existing profile rules; OD72 still refuses empty-enum source declarations. Invalid raw tags do not become valid typed values.
'''+p[b:]
p=p.replace('**Rule:** memory bytes are initialized octets or uninitialized.','''**State choice (question 2):** Core and its Lean evaluator use one hybrid state: structured places for safe typed storage, plus byte/provenance blocks for raw and exposed storage. A partial place-to-block/range projection and Rep relate the two. They are not independently writable copies: typed writes update the represented range; raw writes invalidate affected typed validity until checked decoding restores it. Address-taking materializes a coherent representation with fixed padding choices for that execution. Raw pointers, expose/from_exposed and sizeof/alignof therefore already have Core meanings. Layout is a read-only parameter. Memory CFG makes this representation and ABI explicit for every operation; it does not introduce raw memory for the first time. This keeps typed proofs abstract while allowing slice 24 to execute without replacing Core state.

**Rule:** memory bytes are initialized octets or uninitialized.''')
p=p.replace('cannot affect typed equality, control or observations.','cannot affect typed equality, typed control or typed observations. Initialized padding may be observed through authorized raw byte reads and then affect output. Representation simulations preserve such observed bytes, or explicitly relate the same allowed padding choices; they cannot erase a raw observation as irrelevant nondeterminism.')
p=p.replace('Every access needs live provenance, bounds, initialization, alignment or an explicit unaligned mode, and authority/loan permission.','Nonzero accesses need provenance, bounds, liveness, alignment (or justified unaligned mode), initialization for reads and write permission for writes. Q3.7’s zero-byte raw access needs only alignment and a non-null address.')
p=p.replace('Their accesses still require a valid lifetime and permission.','Typed operations still discharge their logical ownership obligations; this does not strengthen the zero-byte raw-access rule.')
p=p.replace('over eligible exposed blocks.','over live, nonempty eligible exposed blocks.')
p=p.replace('Initialization/padding and pointer-fragment details above are proposed completions of Q3.11, not separately decided rows.','Hybrid state, initialization/padding and pointer-fragment completions require question 2; they do not revise Q3.7’s exception.')
p=p.replace('Entropy is a nullary launch-grant type, with its source a named premise.','Entropy’s source is a named premise under the randomness decision; its exact grant shape and ending contract wait for slice 6 (question 14).')
p=p.replace('erased only when release is statically known and needs no per-instance information.','with any erasure requiring question 6 and a proof that release is statically known and needs no per-instance information.')
p=p.replace('1-based line and byte column (tab counts as one byte);','1-based line and column;')
a=p.index('Propose the total kind rendering'); b=p.index('\\n\\nAn environment response',a)
p=p[:a]+'''The kind inventory/rendering and byte-column/tab convention are questions 9–10. No abbreviated MCU format is introduced.'''+p[b:]
a=p.index('Reserve operation/event parameters'); b=p.index('\\n\\n**Authority:**',a)
p=p[:a]+'''Concurrency is deferred to slice 28. Sequential theorems assume a single thread and no interference. Any future extension must preserve complete executions of that fragment under those premises; deleting synchronization events from interfering executions is insufficient. No concurrent memory model is chosen here.'''+p[b:]
a=p.index('**Rule:** record branch choices'); b=p.index('\\n\\n**Authority:**',a)
p=p[:a]+'''**Rule:** leakage records branch decisions, exact addresses, allocation sizes, loop counts and operands of variable-time operations, with public lengths and contracted external effects. A release record carries ordered (typed site, released value), with reason/path metadata for facts. The release-policy interface lists allowed functions/sites or forbids release, checks reachable releases through calls, and permits only explicit declared values. Its security obligation is delimited release: for equal public inputs, matched external choices and equal ordered release site/value traces, prove equal public observations (NI), and separately equal leakage traces (CT). Policy authorization alone does not establish either theorem or allow other secret effects. These are distinct decided claims, not an optional variable-time Secret tier.

Preserve wipe events as effects optimization cannot erase. Per allocator lineage report requested bytes, net change and peak logical bytes, plus allocator-defined footprint, each a sound bound with status or “not established”. Release/reset conserve accounting, and zero-size ownership adds no invented bytes. Bounds use polynomials with max over counts, lengths and depths; stack remains separate. This is the A2i instrumentation of R-0592, not later slice analysis.
'''+p[b:]
p=p.replace('over instrumented state.','over instrumented hybrid Core state: "'`eval(target, layout, environmentChoices, fuel, coreProgram, state)`. This is the reference evaluator for qualification; it executes Core, not BIR or source text. Choices explicitly cover external responses, allocation/address choices, exposed-pointer alternatives and padding materialization. A choice input can be an oracle rather than a finite stream; each answer is checked against the operation contract. Fixed inputs make evaluation deterministic.'"',1)
p=p.replace('Source struct invariants require construction initialization and preservation by permitted mutation, with module-boundary obligations explicit.','Struct-invariant source rules remain those in the SPARK decision; their implementation waits for slice 17 (question 15).')
p=p.replace('At Memory CFG, lower structured values and calls once to layout, byte/provenance memory and ABI.','At Memory CFG, lower the hybrid Core representation to explicit byte/provenance operations and ABI calls, proving its simulation against C4’s projection.')
p=p.replace('Earlier semantics is layout-independent; representation simulations relate it to this boundary.','Earlier semantics uses C4’s hybrid state and read-only layout interface; representation simulations relate it to this explicit lowering boundary.')
a=p.index('**Native closure (P3):**'); b=p.index('\\n**Qualification (P4):**',a)
p=p[:a]+'''**Certificates and maximal behavior:** retain the [compiler-execution decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): every authoritative compilation emits kernel-checked certificates binding the exact source bytes through independent judgments/validated edges to the exact emitted BIR, and binding the actual backend input to it. A digest establishes identity, not semantic correspondence. Native execution of proved Lean definitions is not itself proof; any gap retains explicit compiler/runtime/toolchain assumptions. After slice 0 use the decided per-edge certificates.

Each lowering proves refinement of maximal behaviors, including termination, abort/trap, blocking and infinite execution with their traces under matched allowed choices. Target steps may stutter finitely; a progress or well-founded argument excludes infinite silent target stuttering that replaces source progress or completion. Q9.9 already calls for maximal behaviors; this makes the proof obligation explicit.

**Native closure (P3):** retain Q8.1’s proved source→BIR, qualified native and conditional image claim, LP0 after slice 1 and R-0505’s later final-image closure. Question 8 separately asks whether to advance it to 0.1. Any validator must bind exact linked image bytes, encodings, relocations, symbols, entry state, launcher/ABI, retained dependencies and target to LCB instruction semantics. Hardware/ISA correspondence, loader/OS/environment, foreign/runtime code, adequate stack and timing/wipe premises remain named unless discharged. LLVM stays unverified.
'''+p[b:]
p=p.replace('run LLVM, LCB and the Lean evaluator','run LLVM, LCB and the hybrid Core Lean evaluator')
p=p.replace('with matched input bytes, grant configuration, external responses and nondeterministic choices.','with matched input bytes, target/layout profile, grant configuration, external responses and allowed nondeterministic choices. Frontend certificates bind this Core program to the source; representation simulations relate its raw-byte/layout observations to both native paths. Compare observable effects, not evaluator internals or raw step counts.')
a=p.index('## 4.'); p=p[:a]+'''## 4. Revision register

No revision is enacted here. Each question in section 5 has its own answer; an umbrella approval cannot choose these independently.

| Rows | Scope and status | Question |
|---|---|---|
| OD126; Q3.7/Q3.9 | Generalize address/profile interface, preserving provenance rules and zero-byte exception; existing 64-bit little-endian profile unchanged. | 3 |
| Q3.5; TYPE-19; FFI-7 | Add usize; replace u64 index/length/sizeof/alignof results only if approved. Fixed-width data unchanged. | 7 |
| OD126; FFI-7 | Native enum tag/payload layout changes existing i32-tag profile separately from target abstraction. | 4 |
| Q1.7 | Proved release-context erasure, including owning Text where applicable; no unconditional one-pointer claim. | 6 |
| Q7.1/Q7.10; Q9.7 | Lawful registry instances rather than a permanently closed inventory; preserve existing grant contracts. Entropy details deferred. | 16, 14 |
| FFI-7/10 | Natural native struct layout already supersedes unspecified-layout prose under runtime decision; retain repr restrictions. | Retained clarification |
| Q8.1; early-evidence decision; R-0505 | Keep current conditional endpoint and later closure; advancing it requires separate approval. | 8 |

Q9.11/OD144 already decide post-cleanup ensures timing. The SPARK decision already decides source struct invariants. Neither is a new rule. C4’s hybrid/representation completion is a proposed formalization choice, not authority to revise raw access. Exact trap constructors, byte columns and conversion errors are not inferred from Q6.7 or ArithmeticError.

## 5. Separately answerable owner questions

Each recommendation is a proposal, not an implemented language decision. “Defer” preserves existing decided rules and leaves detailed instances to the named slice.

1. **Foundation scope.** Recommend approving the interface/law boundary of C1–C10, with freeze only after implementation, kernel proofs, interaction tests, independent review and owner acceptance. Runner-up: freeze smaller integrated scopes incrementally. Neither approves the other numbered questions.
2. **Hybrid state and representation completion.** Recommend C4’s structured-place/byte-block state, coherent projection, pointer fragments and explicit initialization/padding laws. Runner-up: use byte blocks for all Core storage. The hybrid keeps typed proofs small while raw operations remain executable; both must preserve Q3.7 and raw observations.
3. **Target abstraction.** Recommend read-only profiles separating address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; generalize Q3.7/Q3.9 without changing existing profile constants or reconstruction policy. Runner-up: separate semantics per profile under OD126. No usize or enum change is included.
4. **Enum layout.** Recommend retaining the existing profile’s i32 tags for now, deferring a compact u8/u16/u32 algorithm to the aggregate/layout slice and a separate owner answer. Runner-up: approve now the smallest tag fitting the variant count (u8 ≤256, u16 ≤65,536, u32 ≤2"'^32), declaration-order tags, aligned common payload and rounded aggregate size, rejecting larger counts. This changes OD126/FFI-7 independently of question 3.
5. **Niches.** Three options: (a) exactly one null-empty/non-null single-pointer-payload niche, with pointer-size, provenance, ownership and release-context proofs; (b) tagged layout now, future niches only by owner decision; (c) no niches permanently. Recommend **(b)**: the foundation needs representation laws, not a permanent optimization inventory. Runner-up **(a)** gives compact optional ownership but adds a special eligibility proof; multiword release contexts and nullable raw pointers are ineligible. Reviewers split: Kimi preferred (a), Codex offered (b), agy preferred (c). (c) is uniform but unnecessarily permanent.
6. **Release-context erasure.** Recommend retaining sufficient stable context and erasing only with a release/identity/dependency preservation proof and no per-instance state. Runner-up: always store context. This separately revises Q1.7; it does not fix Text’s internal word count.
7. **usize.** Recommend target-sized usize for indices, lengths, capacities, allocation sizes and sizeof/alignof; keep fixed-width integers and add no isize implicitly. Runner-up: retain Q3.5’s u64 with checked target limits. This revises TYPE-19/FFI-7 separately from profile abstraction.
8. **Native-image milestone.** Recommend keeping R-0505’s later closure and Q8.1’s conditional endpoint. Runner-up: require proved exact AArch64 linked-image validation by 0.1, explicitly revising Q8.1, early-evidence and proof-infrastructure milestones while retaining all undischarged premises. Qualification is question 17, not bundled here.
9. **Trap inventory and spellings.** Recommend deferring concrete constructors/renderings until trap semantics needs them, before their first supported slice. Runner-up: approve `arithmetic(ArithmeticError) | bounds | invalidRange | abort`, rendered `overflow`, `division_by_zero`, `invalid_shift`, `bounds`, `invalid_range`, `abort`, with one trailing newline. ArithmeticError’s decided cases do not decide this wrapper or its strings. Preserve the runtime line format and exits either way.
10. **Columns and tabs.** Recommend byte columns, tabs counting one byte, with conformance tests; runner-up: Unicode-scalar columns, tabs counting one scalar. The runtime decision fixes only 1-based columns, so neither convention is already decided.
11. **UTF-8 failure classification.** Recommend deferring mapping to the text slice’s operation contracts while preserving UTF-8 validity and no hidden traps. Runner-up: map unchecked UTF-8 boundary failures to proposed invalidRange. This does not turn handled checked errors into traps.
12. **Checked-conversion error type.** Recommend deciding the type in the integer/conversion slice before exposing the API. Runner-up: a distinct builtin ConversionError with OutOfRange, returned as Result<T, ConversionError>, outside ArithmeticError and TrapKind. No unplaced OutOfRange constructor is frozen.
13. **Destruction order.** Recommend deferring arrays and collections to their slices and checked Destroy bodies. Runner-up: freeze increasing array indices then backing, and ascending OrderedMap keys, key before value then nodes. The foundation needs sequencing/cleanup preservation, not these library orders.
14. **Entropy shape.** Recommend deferring exact launch-grant type, operations and ending contract to slice 6, consistent with Q7.10 and the randomness schedule. Runner-up: approve a nullary Entropy grant only together with a complete instance contract. No nullary shape is assumed by C5.
15. **Struct-invariant implementation.** Recommend deferring implementation to slice 17 while retaining the already-decided SPARK boundary rules and Q9.11/OD144 post-cleanup ensures semantics. Runner-up: implement those same decided rules earlier if an interaction proof needs them. There is no new timing choice to approve.
16. **Registry extensibility.** Recommend lawful typed extensions preserving existing primitive/grant entries and exhaustive coverage of every selected instance; unknown operations are refused. Runner-up: retain closed Q9.7/Q7.10 inventories and seek an owner amendment for each addition. Schema compatibility must preserve old records; no new source spelling is implied.
17. **Differential qualification.** Recommend P4’s complete-observation comparison on every supported controlled example/test across LLVM, LCB and explicit-choice Core evaluation. Runner-up: staged coverage with every unsupported/inconclusive case reported and no stronger correctness claim. Preserve the already-decided two-backend comparison; testing never replaces proof.
'"'''
Path('_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md').write_text(p)
