- <a id="decided-project-spark-2026-11-06"></a>**What Concrete takes from SPARK** (owner, 2026-11-06: "ok do everything", after Claude, Codex, agy and Kimi chose unanimously; the
  [review](proposals/SPARK.md) items 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 9 and 10).
  - *Function values with safety preconditions.* A function whose `#[requires]` is a safety
    precondition is refused as a function value, including through wrappers and specialization. A
    binding task designs function-type contracts, trait methods included, as a prerequisite for
    accepting slice 20 (function values), or earlier if a workload pulls it (R-0601); it is never
    broadened to non-safety contracts.
  - *Struct invariants.* `#[invariant(...)]` on a struct with private fields holds at every
    exposure. The definition is written in the spec before the freeze. Revised 2026-11-07 (owner:
    "ok go ahead all yes then as you said right", approving the recorded recommendation): the source
    feature moves from the structs slice (slice 9) to the modules slice (slice 17), where field
    privacy exists.
  - *Facts at a point.* `assert(e) by thm(...)` extends [OD79](#od79). Its role is strictly
    separate from `#[proof]`: `#[proof]` discharges the function's obligations, `assert(e) by
    thm(...)` establishes one fact at one point, and the theorem's hypotheses are fully discharged.
  - *Observable events.* Core gets an extensible observable-event kind now, which also protects
    secret wipes (R-0602); device semantics come later.
  - *Unused permissions.* A permission that justifies nothing is a fact that a `Concrete.toml`
    policy may refuse; the decided refusals (an unmatched `#[proof]`, a duplicate
    `#[may_not_terminate]`) stay.
  - *Measures.* Lexicographic measures come when a workload needs them (R-0605).
  - *Assurance.* Assurance is a set of separate named, versioned properties per module, each with
    its scope, evidence and coverage, not a ladder (R-0606).
- <a id="decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06"></a>**Trap line, default layout and spec version** (owner, 2026-11-06: "ok do everything", after Claude, Codex, agy and Kimi chose unanimously).
  - *The trap line* ([OD101](#od101)'s form, spec obligation O4): `concrete: trap: <kind> at
    <path>:<line>:<col> (site <id>)`, the path relative to the project root with forward slashes,
    line and column 1-based.
  - *Default struct layout:* declaration order, natural alignment defined exactly per target, the
    size rounded up to the alignment, and zero-sized fields taking no bytes. It is not a C ABI
    promise (R-0502).
  - *Spec version:* every output carries one `spec_version`, in one header covering every record
1:# Concrete Lean foundation proposal — draft 2
3:Status: proposal, not a completed freeze or approval of new rules. On 2026-11-07 the owner said, verbatim, **"ok to option b then for freeze the foundation>?"** (owner answer supplied with this revision). This approves writing the shared foundation before freezing it; **pending recording in docs/decisions.md**. It does not approve the new questions in section 5.
9:## 1. The freeze boundary
15:Before freezing, implement these contracts in the authoritative Lean library, with readable comments, accepted/refused examples and counterexamples to invalid premises. Discharge the four Q9.14 design theorems and the [A2i interaction/erasure obligations](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06), including the laws below. Tests alone do not establish them. Existing `ConcreteSpec` fragments are starting material, not evidence that omitted pointer fragments, external frames, initialization or cleanup interactions are already covered. Review and owner approval precede the freeze. This is a writing proposal, not that completed review or approval.
17:## 2. Contracts to finish before the freeze
21:### C1. Execution, calls and cleanup
31:### C2. Scalars, failure and arithmetic
33:**Freeze:** mathematical scalar values, finite bit representations, operation contracts and error classification; representation and operation correspondence laws. Integer widths remain those named by `i8`…`i64` and `u8`…`u64`; proposed `usize` is separate. A bool has only false/true, encoded as byte 0/1. TYPE-20 makes char a one-byte code unit: every u8 round-trips through char. OD137 restricts character literals to ASCII; it does not restrict stored char values to ASCII.
42:### C3. Target, layout and representation
56:### C4. Byte memory and value validity
70:### C5. Dynamic borrow, brands and authority
76:Fresh brand binders are nominal, including reset/loop generations; dependencies cannot escape their lifetime. Child allocators own backing and expose parent dependence. Grant narrowing consumes and intersects rights; acquisition preserves the parent as decided; close/give-up consumes its resource on all specified outcomes. Zero representation size never removes linear obligations. Permissions in `with(...)` are erased sets, distinct from runtime grants. `GrantTy` describes launch-grant types; File/Socket/Listener are acquired handles. Clock kind has separate identity/resolution/guarantee metadata. Entropy’s source is a named premise under the randomness decision; its exact grant shape and ending contract wait for slice 6 (question 14).
82:### C6. Observations, environment and concurrency extension
92:**Authority:** Core Q6.7, Q7.5–Q7.10, Q8.4–Q8.6; [runtime](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06), [stack bounds](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness), [concurrency last](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-final-slices). The extension/projection laws are proposed contracts. **Later:** grant-specific events and adapters; concurrency and atomic laws at slice 28, last. Public environment effects are not deterministic merely because input replay is deterministic.
94:### C7. Secret leakage and accounting
105:### C8. Evaluator adequacy and termination
113:### C9. Identities, primitive contracts, evidence and ghost erasure
123:### C10. Frames and ABI
131:## 3. Pipeline and validation contract
137:BIR feeds the LLVM printer or the proved BIR→LCB adapter. **Memory CFG stays the single layout/ABI/provenance lowering boundary**; retract P2's folding proposal. Earlier semantics uses C4’s hybrid state and read-only layout interface; representation simulations relate it to this explicit lowering boundary.
151:## 4. Revision register
161:| Q7.1/Q7.10; Q9.7 | Lawful registry instances rather than a permanently closed inventory; preserve existing grant contracts. Entropy details deferred. | 16, 14 |
165:Q9.11/OD144 already decide post-cleanup ensures timing. The SPARK decision already decides source struct invariants. Neither is a new rule. C4’s hybrid/representation completion is a proposed formalization choice, not authority to revise raw access. Exact trap constructors, byte columns and conversion errors are not inferred from Q6.7 or ArithmeticError.
167:## 5. Separately answerable owner questions
169:Each recommendation is a proposal, not an implemented language decision. “Defer” preserves existing decided rules and leaves detailed instances to the named slice.
179:9. **Trap inventory and spellings.** Recommend deferring concrete constructors/renderings until trap semantics needs them, before their first supported slice. Runner-up: approve `arithmetic(ArithmeticError) | bounds | invalidRange | abort`, rendered `overflow`, `division_by_zero`, `invalid_shift`, `bounds`, `invalid_range`, `abort`, with one trailing newline. ArithmeticError’s decided cases do not decide this wrapper or its strings. Preserve the runtime line format and exits either way.
180:10. **Columns and tabs.** Recommend byte columns, tabs counting one byte, with conformance tests; runner-up: Unicode-scalar columns, tabs counting one scalar. The runtime decision fixes only 1-based columns, so neither convention is already decided.
181:11. **UTF-8 failure classification.** Recommend deferring mapping to the text slice’s operation contracts while preserving UTF-8 validity and no hidden traps. Runner-up: map unchecked UTF-8 boundary failures to proposed invalidRange. This does not turn handled checked errors into traps.
184:14. **Entropy shape.** Recommend deferring exact launch-grant type, operations and ending contract to slice 6, consistent with Q7.10 and the randomness schedule. Runner-up: approve a nullary Entropy grant only together with a complete instance contract. No nullary shape is assumed by C5.
